  "narrative": {
    "intro": "At and T Enterprises obligated $229M in FY2027 to date across 24 awards, with an average award size of $9.5M. Obligations are down 77.9% year over year. FY obligations peaked in FY25 and FY26 at $1B each before falling to $229M in FY27. The Department of Veterans Affairs provided the bulk of FY2027 work, obligating $226M. Wired Telecommunications Carriers is the primary market for FY2027 work, with $229M in obligations. Smaller FY2027 buyers include the Department of Justice at $1.6M and the Department of Defense at $921K. Several agencies show little or no FY2027 spend with At and T Enterprises, including the General Services Administration, the Department of the Treasury, the Environmental Protection Agency, and the Department of Transportation, each at $0. The historical record shows growth from $349M in FY22 to $778M in FY23, $941M in FY24, and $1B in each of FY25 and FY26 before the FY27 decline. This profile reflects a VA-heavy, wired-telecom-focused federal portfolio in the current fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the main buyers of At and T Enterprises in FY2027 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is the main buyer, obligating $226M. The Department of Justice and the Department of Defense follow with $1.6M and $921K respectively. Other agencies buying in FY2027 include the United States Trade and Development Agency at $105K and the Department of Homeland Security at $27.5K; several agencies recorded $0."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How has At and T Enterprises’ federal business trended over recent fiscal years?",
        "body": "Obligations rose from $349M in FY22 to $778M in FY23 and $941M in FY24, reached $1B in both FY25 and FY26, then declined to $229M in FY27. Year over year obligations fell 77.9% into the current fiscal year. The Wired Telecommunications Carriers market accounted for $229M of FY2027 obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
